#### Sunlight Bazaar

> **Adventure Seed: The Cursed Woman**
>
> Hook Hafsa bint Shadah appears to be under the effects of a dangerous curse! First, a heavy clay pot fell and narrowly missed her head. Then, a deadly serpent was found in her bedroom! Hafsa’s daughter, Ulma, requests assistance in banishing the evil spirits or malicious djinn who are plaguing her mother, who refuses to believe in “superstitious nonsense.”
>
> Rising Action The so-­called “curse” is nothing of the kind. Rather, one of Hafsa’s debtors is desperately trying to kill her before his loan is due. Nasu, an Ujik from a wealthy family, took out a large loan ostensibly to buy horses to trade in al-Zawira. Instead, he drank and gambled away most of the money in the Lucky Donkey. Now he is in debt not only to Hafsa but to the White Lady as well. When the PCs find him, Nasu attempts to hire them as bodyguards, with vague promises of future wealth and favors.
>
> Climax Nasu gains an unexpected reprieve and a worrisome ally: the White Lady has her own reasons to want Hafsa dead and has offered to forgive Nasu’s debt if he arranges the deed. Nasu steps up his attempts at murder by setting Hafsa’s coffee house on fire, which may have dangerous repercussions for innocent bystanders or the PCs! Nasu’s incompetence threatens the White Lady, so she sends her own much more competent killers to clean things up and leave Nasu as the fall guy. Any unfortunate witnesses (like the PCs) will have to be eliminated as well.

#### Evening Flower Teahouse

#### The Grove of the Dead

In the center of the Grove, on the highest, driest ground, lie the yurts of the priests. Any priests who happen to be in the city are invited to take up residence in the Grove, so the number of tents and people dwelling within changes day to day. The most reliable and revered priest in the Grove is the wizened Ujin Dayan herself, so gaunt that with her white face paint on, she resembles one of the skulls that she chatters with day in and day out. Dayan has forgotten more about medicine, herbs, and poisons than most people ever learn, and she has a fearsome reputation as a diviner as well—fearsome because unlike some, she never shies away from delivering bad news along with the good.

#### Calamac Ordu

> **Adventure Seed: The Dark Horse**
>
> Hook The Calamac ordu has recently come into possession of a superior black stallion, which it was able to buy very cheaply. But misfortune has followed after—Calamac Tygan was thrown and broke his leg, and another Calamac cousin, Gorgu, died mysteriously in the night, his broken body found behind his yurt. Fearing a curse, or worse, treachery from the Menghes family, the Calamacs may reach out to PCs with reputations as diplomats or investigators.
>
> Rising Action Tracing the origin of the mysterious stallion reveals that it was, in fact, sold by the Menghes family to the Calamac family via an ignorant go-between. But Menghes Benve claims that the horse simply appeared in their herd one day and had been such a menace to any rider or even passerby that she had tried to get rid of it quickly. She knows nothing of any curse or any deaths. Meanwhile, signs of some animal attempting to gain access to the Calamac family shrine have been found all around the shrine’s yurt, despite the mysterious horse being firmly locked away in a paddock all night.
>
> Climax The horse is a spirit, either a reincarnation or an echo of Menghes Orojin. (Use the characteristics of a Wind Horse, page 156, with the Trickster template [see the core rulebook, page 311]; the horse can mask its supernatural nature easily, especially during the day.) Angry at both the Menghes and Calamac families, the Orojin-horse is attempting to gain access to the swords it bore in life, stored in the Calamac shrine. It will kill to do so and take its revenge on those who fought alongside Orojin and allowed him to die in battle with barbarians. There is only one person in the ordu whom the horse will neither trouble nor disobey: Orojin’s former betrothed, Calamac Alma. If the PCs do not intervene, the horse-spirit will eventually cause such chaos that Alma will enter the temple (which the horse-spirit cannot do) and bring out the swords, at which point the horse will steal the swords and ride off into the Spirit Realms, never to be seen again.

#### The Border Stones

#### The White Guard

#### The Scarlet Banners

Primarily comprising nomadic Ujik from the Moto family and their vassals, the Scarlet Banners are ill at ease in the city that is their responsibility. This mingghan of the White Horde is second only to the White Guard in its access to warriors and matériel, but many of its rank and file would gladly exchange their posting for one less prestigious. In the opinion of Moto Rurame Noyan, the Scarlet Banners’ commander, the Moto do not belong in cities—and it is an opinion many of her warriors share.

### The Qamarist Caliphate

#### The Lands of the Caliphate

To the north lie the windswept plains that slope gently down to the still, black waters of the Cursed Sea. This is the home of the Suhili, the people of the plains, proud horse lords of the Caliphate. Despite the proximity of the Cursed Sea, the plains in this region are as fertile as any other and make excellent grazing lands for horses, cattle, goats, and sheep. Small streams that flow through the plains are shaded by enormous chenar trees, prized for their shade-giving crowns in the heat of the summer. Yet for all its charms, the area is feared and avoided by most Qamari—for it is where monsters dwell. The villages that do dot the area are crammed behind tall earthen walls, dwellings and workshops piled atop one another in the smallest footprint available as groves, fields, and farmlands surround them. The monsters that crawl from the sea are often swift, and even walls are no barrier to the dragons and other winged beasts, so Suhili go nowhere unarmed, and all their settlements are vigorously defended. Their mounted patrols ride swiftly to any word of a new monster, hunting and killing the beast as quickly as can be managed. Despite these precautions, everyone has at least one family member who has been slain by the servants of Zeth, and the hatred of the Drowned God burns brightly in the people here.

### Families of the Unicorn

#### Five Winds, One Clan

- Lady Shinjo forbade her followers from marrying in-tribe. For instance, an Utaku could not marry another Utaku, and an Ide could not marry another Ide, but an Utaku and Ide could marry, assuming they did not share lineages. As such, Unicorn samurai refuse marriage arrangements if there is a family connection, even generations removed, and even if there is no blood relation. Because marriage is traditionally how the Rokugani finalize treaties, this sometimes creates headaches for Ide marriage arrangers. Most Unicorn will not disobey Lady Shinjo for something as trite as political gain (to the Ide’s chagrin).
- Unicorn samurai rarely retire, more often undertaking the kurichitai tradition, which dates back to the time the clan wandered the lands. When an elderly samurai decides it is their time, they pack their horse with minimal provisions and journey into the desert, never to return. Considered their “final adventure,” it is seen as an honorable way to part from the Mortal Realm, akin to a Lion samurai dying in battle. While this is a controversial practice outside the clan, it is culturally regarded as a spiritually important—if somber—final act. Even so, elders are not pressured into this tradition, and many never undertake the kurichitai at all.
- The Unicorn have incorporated many customs and mannerisms from other cultures. They shake hands instead of bowing, have no taboo against public loudness, and generally accept public displays of affection, to name a few. However, just as they observed the customs of the lands they visited during their travels, so too do Unicorn adopt the customs of Rokugani provinces they visit, so as to be respectful of their hosts. Even so, there are a few slipups from time to time.

