##### The Golden Yurts of the Ide

#### The Moto Family Mon

#### The original heraldry of the Moto family was a red camellia, symbolizing fearlessness in the face of death. The flower was depicted with ten petals, one for each of the Lords of Death. Now, the Moto display a mon that highlights the distinictive face paint for which they are known.

#### Culture and Traditions

##### Religious Beliefs

The religious branches of Rokugan do not quite know what to make of the Moto’s spiritual practices. Because proximity to death is considered spiritually staining and a source of misfortune, Fortunists and priests of the elemental spirits regard the Lords of Death as vengeful spirits to be avoided, if they accept the religious practice at all. The Brotherhood of Shinsei is somewhat more accepting, as Shinsei, too, taught that death was merely a new beginning. Such monks rationalize the Lords of Death as beings caught in a state of worldly transition, lingering until such a time as they can pass into Meido, the Realm of Waiting, where the deceased await judgment. Isawa scholars of the Phoenix attempt to reconcile the Lords of Death as lesser servants of Emma-Ō, the Fortune of Death, or perhaps a few of his judges. Others are quick to point out that the Moto gods are also their blessed ancestors, and that veneration of one’s ancestors is expected of all samurai. The Moto are content to allow outsiders to speculate however they wish. What matters to them is not the perceptions of others but being true to themselves.

#### The Lands of the Moto

#### Moto Syujiko

Companions: Two camels (see Camel in Path of Waves: The Essential Guide to Rōnin, page 235)

Gear (other): Wakizashi (short sword), traveling supplies, divination kit, various trinkets, string of koku

## CHAPTER 2

## The Blessings of the Sands

> **ANIMAL SPIRITS AND YŌKAI**
>
> Most animals and animal spirits conceptualize the Celestial Order as follows: demons ascend to become animals, animals ascend to become yōkai yōkai (supernatural beings) or animal spirits (like tengu, kitsu, kitsune, and so forth), yōkai and animal spirits ascend to become humans, and humans ascend to become spirits or Fortunes.

### The Fox Clan

#### Palace of the Fox

Visitors expecting a backwater folksy community are taken aback upon arriving at Kyūden Kitsune. The Castle of the Fox is every bit the distinguished palace, offering all the expected amenities of a true kyūden. This traditional, seven-story castle has even been the site of several Imperial Winter Courts, offering an oasis of urban comforts in the rustic wilderness. The surrounding town, while modest, is likewise similar to any other forest community, except perhaps for the pointed lack of loggers. Bushi often remark that both the castle and its town are not very defensible, and yet those who live here do not seem particularly concerned about this.

#### Bending to the Woods

The Fox have no such qualms. Their children play in the woods alongside spirits and animals alike (with supervision), and shapeshifting tricksters come and go as they please. A Fox samurai might start their day training in the dōjō of Kyūden Kitsune and end it performing an inscrutable task in the deep woods for a raccoon dog. The Fox make compromises other samurai would not; personal integrity and clout always seem to come second to basic survival. But then, survival is what the Fox do best. They have endured generations of hardship and grown from it. They embrace challenge and are willing to do what it takes to survive. Their outlook is summarized by a colloquial mantra: “Fall down seven times, stand up eight.”

#### Allies and Rivalries

#### Kitsune Ryōsei

Gear (equipped): Sanctified robes (Physical 1, Supernatural 3, Ceremonial), wakizashi (short sword), spiritual offerings, personal chop

#### Fox Clan Player Characters

#### The Kitsune Family

Skill Increase: +1 Fitness, +1 Theology

Members of the Kitsune family are partially descended from shapeshifting foxes and enjoy a close connection with forest denizens. They tend to be graceful, charming, quirky, and a little rustic. Many have green or yellow eyes and features that suggest their animal lineage. Outgoing on the surface, the Kitsune are nonetheless slow to trust outsiders. They remember the hardships they endured due to Great Clan machinations, and while they no longer dwell on what they have lost, they have learned the hard way that nature neither follows humanity’s laws nor cares for its fate. They are most at home in the thickest woods, surrounded by nature and far from populous human civilization.

