### New Advantages and Disadvantages

#### Stargazing (Void)

Effects: The following apply to a character with the Stargazing passion:

#### Animal Signs (Void)

- You assign supernatural meaning to animal behavior. Not all of this behavior portends doom or bad luck, however, so you have a broad range of knowledge on superstitions and methods of fortune-telling related to animals.

#### Disdain for Urban Sprawl (Earth)

Effects: The following apply to a character with the Disdain for Urban Sprawl anxiety:

- You do not like dense urban areas (though small towns are fine) due to a lack of plentiful nature, the crowds of people, the pace of life, or other reasons. You are not necessarily “wild,” “feral,” or “uncivilized” and may even be knowledgeable about city life. It simply brings you no joy.

#### Tip of the Tongue (Air)

Effects: The following apply to a character with the Tip of the Tongue anxiety:

### Table 2-1: New Samurai Heritages

| ROLL | RESULT | MODIFIERS | OTHER EFFECTS |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Ancestral Horse Line: One of your ancestors possessed a mighty Unicorn warhorse, and now your family’s line and the horse’s line parallel each other. | Increase your glory by 3. | You add an additional warhorse to your starting items. Roll a ten-sided die again to determine the horse’s original family (1–2: Utaku horse, 3–4: Shinjo horse, 5–6: Iuchi horse, 7–8: Ide horse, 9–10: Moto horse). New horse options can be found beginning on page 141. |
| 2 | Heart of the Horse: You did not choose your horse: it chose you. Now you have a supernatural bond with it. It has a mind of its own, however, so your relationship with the horse is reciprocal.<br>Treat it well, and it will treat you well. | Increase your glory by 3 and decrease your status by 3. | You gain a horse (if you did not start with one), and you gain the Karmic Tie (Void) distinction (see page 108 of the core rulebook) with it. |
| 3-4 | Knowledge Exchange: One of your ancestors learned the techniques of another school and has passed that knowledge down the generations, finally to you. | Decrease your glory by 5. | You know one additional technique. Roll a ten-sided die again to determine the category (1–3: kata, 4–6: shūji, 7: ritual, 8: invocation, 9: kihō, 10: mahō or ninjutsu), then select one technique with a prerequisite of school rank 1 and learn that technique.<br>You can perform that technique even if it is not normally allowed by your school. |
| 5 | Lost Banner: Your family once possessed a banner that symbolically carries your family’s spirit, but an ancestor lost it due to war, betrayal, deceit, or incompetence. Everyone in your family has sworn to find it. | Decrease your glory by 5. | You gain the Indomitable Will distinction (see page 106 of the core rulebook). This can be assigned in excess of the normal limitations on advantages at character creation. |
| 6-7 | Splintered Loyalties: Either your family is directly related to a non-Rokugani people; or you grew up in Khanbulak, on the border of Rokugan; or you were raised on the Plains of Wind and Stone.<br>Your feelings of loyalty lie equally with the Unicorn and the Empire, but you also have some loyalty to this non-Rokugani group. | Decrease your status by 5. | Roll a ten-sided die again to determine the skill you learned from your other culture (1–2: Aesthetics, 3–4 Courtesy, 5–6: Culture, 7–8: Meditation, 9–10: Commerce). You gain +1 rank in that skill. |
| 8 | Sacred Wilderness: One of your ancestors established an area on your family’s lands as a protected, sacred wilderness. | Increase your status by 5. | You add an estate to your starting items somewhere within your clan’s territory. When at your estate, you and your allies receive food, shelter, and medical care for free. |
| 9 | Spirit Companion: One of your ancestors made an agreement with a spirit for service, and that spirit has served your family for generations. | Increase your status by 3 and decrease your glory by 3. | You know one additional meishōdō talisman. Roll a ten-sided die again to determine the ring of the spirit (1–2: Air, 3–4: Water, 5–6: Earth, 7–8: Fire, 9–10: Void). Then select one invocation of that ring type with a prerequisite of school rank 1 and learn that invocation. You can perform that invocation even if it is not normally allowed by your school. |
| 10 | Spiritual Debt: Your family owes a long-standing debt to a spirit. The spirit occasionally asks your family to perform tasks that may seem incomprehensible or even contrary to what your family, your clan, or the Empire wishes. | Increase your honor by 3 and decrease your glory by 3. | Roll a ten-sided die again to determine the spirit’s ring (1–2: Fire, 3–4: Earth, 5–6: Water, 7–8: Air, 9–10:<br>Void). You may increase the value of your matching ring by 1 and reduce the value of a different ring by 1 (this still cannot raise a ring above 3). |

