### New Techniques

#### General Kata

##### Cautious Tread Rank 1

Horses and camels are excellent mounts, but their long legs make them somewhat more vulnerable to certain kinds of terrain than their human riders. A skillful handler knows how to lead their mount around the most dangerous spots, whether holes dug by rodents in the plains, swampy terrain, or chasms in the desert.

Activation: When you make a Movement action check while riding a mount, you may spend (op) as follows: (op) (op): Choose one of the following terrain qualities: Dangerous, Entangling, or Obscuring. You and your mount ignore its effects. This effect persists until the end of your next turn.

##### Stalking Leopard Style Rank 2

The Ujik hunt and herd as their way of life, and one of the deadliest animals in their domain with which they must contend is the leopard. Silent and precise in its strikes, this creature is emulated by some of the most skillful Ujik hunters.

##### Sudden Downpour Style Rank 3

Like the rains sweeping across the steppe, the warrior rides down their foe with the full power of their mount behind their strikes.

##### Swaying Grass Evasion Rank 3

Whether traveling the Plains of Wind and Stone or riding the Sand Road, there is no more dangerous place to be caught than out in the open without a mount. Warriors experienced in fighting in these regions know to watch for key pieces of terrain, as even a small amount of cover can mean the difference between life and being shot full of arrows.

Activation: As a Movement and Support action, if you are in Dangerous, Entangling, or Obscuring terrain, you may receive a number of fatigue up to your ranks in Survival to duck into cover to avoid oncoming threats.

Effects: Increase the TN of Martial Arts [Ranged] checks targeting you by half the amount of fatigue you received this way (rounded up). This effect persists until the end of your next turn.

#### Close-Combat Kata

##### Distracting Flurry Style Rank 2

The land itself can be a weapon to a warrior who chooses to see the opportunities it presents. Snapping brush, loose soil kicked up by a strike, or even sand hurled in desperation can catch a foe off guard, creating a brief moment of vulnerability.

##### Scouring Wind Style Rank 3

##### Slashing Sandstorm Style Rank 2

#### Ranged Kata

##### Descending Swarm Shot Rank 3

Just as when one hornet stings its foe, the swarm often follows, so too does the warrior’s first shot lead that of their compatriots. By firing at a key weak point or making allies aware of a target, the leader of a squad can focus their efforts, making the entire unit much more deadly.

Activation: As an Attack action using a readied bow or sling, you may make a TN 3 Martial Arts [Ranged] (Air) check targeting one character within range of the weapon. Reduce the TN of this check by 1 if your target is of silhouette 3 or larger.

Effects: If you succeed, your target becomes vulnerable to attacks by your comrades. When a character makes an Attack action check targeting the affected target, if they succeed, they add one bonus success. This effect persists until the end of your next turn.

##### Thunderbolt Style Rank 4

Hitting a target with a thrown weapon is difficult; hitting more than one target with the same throw is even harder. However, a warrior of sufficient skill can potentially harm multiple targets with a properly placed spear or ax throw, the projectile striking one foe and caroming off at an unexpected angle or causing the primary target to collapse into another. Such an unexpected tactic can catch an entire unit off guard, forcing them to spread out when staying close together would be advantageous.

#### New Rituals

##### Center the World Rank 3

The world is a vast and varied place. If one travels far enough, even the stars above become largely unfamiliar. But there are some constants: Lady Sun begins her journey in the east and ends in the west; moss can be found on certain sides of trees; a few stars are fixed no matter where one travels; and human activities have certain key signs.

Effects: If you succeed, you find a sign of the nearest habitation of humans or other sentient beings, and you are able to determine the direction you must travel to reach it.

##### Cultural Exchange Rank 1

Ide Emissaries are trained not only in the customs of known cultures but also in the art of making contact with unknown ones. By presenting elements of their own traditions to others, seeing what people react to, and encouraging other participants to share their own culture, a skillful diplomat can break the ice of first contact, helping multiple disparate groups to see one another as people with much in common despite their differences.

##### Protection of the Flock Rank 2

Effects: If you succeed, you fortify your targets against disease and other hardship. Until the end of the next scene, they use your ranks in Medicine for any checks they make to resist effects.

##### Shadow of Days Rank 3

##### Spiritual Survey Rank 2

##### Traveler’s Experience Rank 2

##### Wayfinder’s Instincts Rank 2

#### Air Shūji

##### Cunning Omission Rank 3

It is said by some courtiers that the easiest way to get someone to admit something is not through blackmail or coercion, but simply by explaining the situation to them incorrectly and letting their impulse to set the record straight fill in the details. By leaving out a key element in a story or account, a courtier attempts to draw their opponent into providing it themself, thus undermining their own argument or offering up the testimony needed to prove the initial courtier’s point.

Activation: As a Scheme action, you may make a Sentiment (Air) check targeting another character. The TN of this check is equal to your target’s focus minus their current strife, to a minimum of 1.

##### Entice with Offerings Rank 3

Activation: As a Scheme action, you may make a TN 3 Commerce (Air) check targeting another character. When you use this technique you must give up something you know the target wants: this could mean giving up a physical object your target desires, or making a concession in a negotiation or argument that adds 2 momentum points to your target’s current objective.

Effects: If you succeed, you distract your target for one round; during that time, your target cannot add momentum to their social objectives. This effect persists for one round, plus one additional round for every two bonus successes.

##### Trader’s Intuition Rank 2

Activation: As a Scheme action, you may make a Commerce (Air) check targeting an object you can observe. The TN of the check is 2, or 4 if the object has the Forbidden item quality.

Effects: If you succeed, you assess the target, learning its monetary value and rarity.

#### Earth Shūji

##### Cite the Facts Rank 2

##### Solidify Gratitude Rank 2

Activation: As a Scheme action, you may make a TN 2 Courtesy (Earth) check targeting another character. You can only target a character who has accepted a gift from you this scene or has completed a social objective to which you contributed momentum points.

Effects: If you succeed, you gain the Ally (Target’s Name) [Water] advantage (page 101 of the core rulebook) unless your target chooses to forfeit glory equal to the rarity of the gift you gave or to the number of momentum points you contributed to their objective.

#### Fire Shūji

##### Glorious Entrance Rank 2

##### Incite Wrath Rank 3

#### Water Shūji

##### Call to Ride Rank 1

##### Complementary Experience Rank 3

Effects: If you succeed, your target has your assistance on the next check they make. This effect persists until the end of your next turn. If you succeed, your target may grant you assistance on the next check you make. This effect persists until the end of your next turn.

##### Everyone Has a Price Rank 4

##### Horse Whisperer Rank 3

Activation: As a Support action, you may make a Courtesy (Water) or Survival (Water) check targeting a horse or other equine creature that can see or hear you and trusts you. The TN of this check is the target’s vigilance.

Effects: If you succeed, you make a wordless request to the target, which they comprehend and are compelled to fulfill. If what they are doing is helpful to your action or to another character’s action, that action counts as having assistance. You cannot request something impossible for the target to accomplish. You may put them in harm’s way, but if they become injured or harmed, it breaks the trust, and they become immune to this shūji.

#### Void Shūji

##### Appreciate the Scenery Rank 1

#### New Water Invocation

##### The World is Not Heavy Rank 2

## CHAPTER 3

## The Gifts of the World

### Currency in the Cradle of the World

#### Table 3-1: Trade Goods along the Sand Road

| COMMODITY | RARITY | LOW PRICE RANGE | HIGH PRICE RANGE | NOTES |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| FOOD AND DRINK |  |  |  |  |
| Street food | 1 | 4 zeni or 3 quarter-qanars | 6 zeni or 5 quarter-qanars | In every city are stalls selling cheap food, and along the Burning Sands, it is no different. From fried chickpea patties served wrapped in flatbread to shish kebabs of spiced vegetables to a local delicacy of grilled sheep’s-milk cheese, there is no end to the delectable delicacies. |
| Food served at inns or restaurants | 1 | 5 zeni or 1 qanar | 8 zeni or one and a half qanars | In inns and restaurants throughout the Caliphate, it is common to see fava bean and chickpea soup; flatbreads topped with fresh, aromatic herbs; and grape leaves stuffed with rice and vegetables. |
| Dessert | 2 | 3 zeni or 2 quarter-qanars | 6 zeni or 5 quarter-qanars | At street stalls and inns, you can find buttery pastry topped with honey and nuts; candied cherries and pomegranate seeds; and a cake-like dish made from sweet sheep’s-milk cheese and rose- or orange-blossom water. |
| Suutai tsai tea | 3 | 2 zeni or 1 quarter-qanar | 13 zeni or 5 half-qanars | Often served in Ujik settlements, this tea is made from milk, tea leaves, and salt. This drink is also used as a base for soups with the addition of vegetables and dumplings. |
| Arkhi | 1 (among the Ujik); otherwise 6 | 4 zeni or 3 quarter-qanars | 6 zeni or 5 quarter-qanars | While the Caliphate bans alcohol, the Ujik have no such qualms about producing and consuming liquor. This drink is made from alcohol and fermented mare’s milk.<br>It is commonly found among the Ujik and at inns that serve them. |
| Arak | 6 | 2 zeni or 1 quarter-qanar | 13 zeni or 5 half-qanars | While alcohol is forbidden by the laws of the Caliphate, the hidden sale of liquor remains, often under the guise of a medicine or elixir. This liquor is one such item. Made from grapes and anise seed, it has a strong licorice or herbal taste. Its milky-white appearance and strong potency have given it the nickname lion’s milk. |
| Sea-buckthorn juice | 2 | 2 zeni or 1 quarter-qanar | 13 zeni or 5 half-qanars | The sea buckthorn is a hearty plant that bears orange fruit around the size of a thumb. It is popular as one of the only fruits that grow naturally in the rockier parts of the Burning Sands. |
| CLOTHING |  |  |  |  |
| Nehiri robes | 2 | 1 koku or 1 gold shenar | 3 koku or 3 gold shenars | These loose-fitting robes are perfect for comfortable wear in the desert heat and come in bright, eye-catching colors.<br>They are often worn with thick, beaded belts and special pointed shoes known as balga. |
| Bandar kaftan and leather overlay | 6 | 5 koku or 5 gold shenars | 10 koku or 10 gold shenars | These loose, neutral-colored kaftans are worn with sturdy, camel-leather overlays. The overlays are often tiled like a samurai’s armor or cut into scalelike pieces. This style of clothing was developed for wear in Bandar trade caravans, allowing the wearer to blend in with the sands while the leather overlay adds protection and offers a convenient place to discreetly hide weapons. |
| Sogdan coat | 6 | 10 koku or 10 gold shenars | 30 koku or 30 gold shenars | These elegantly patterned coats are particularly eye-catching, incorporating bright colors and intricate embroideries. It is common to see Sogdan coats in places of high learning such as libraries, courts, and academic institutions, where the more elaborate the coat, the higher educated the wearer is assumed to be. |

